    author "codex"
    created_at 2026-08-14
}

record lege-ecosystem.papercut.collated-208-papercuts-from-akr-evidence-intake/1 : papercut {
    title "Collated 208 papercuts from AKR, Evidence-intake-project, Kitchen-Concept, SaveYourSkin and 4 more"
    state verified
    statement """
        Collated 208 papercuts from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects on 2026-08-16 (D-030). Each source key is also in the collated slot, which is the dedup set for the next run; the sisters were read and never written.

        ## AKR @akr.papercut.after-an-immediate-akr-check-akr-77-records-88
        After , an immediate akr check — akr 77 records, 88 revisions, 6 files commit (none), grammar 0.1, vocabulary 0.2, today 2026-08-11 stages A-D                                 ok stage F  emit (in memory)   7 views        FAILED ROADMAP.md               DIFFERS CURRENT-STATE.md         DIFFERS ACTIVE-WORK.md           DIFFERS REVIEW-REQUIRED.md       DIFFERS OPEN-QUESTIONS.md        DIFFERS DECISION-HISTORY.md      DIFFERS PAPERCUTS.md             DIFFERS error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/ACTIVE-WORK.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/CURRENT-STATE.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/DECISION-HISTORY.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/OPEN-QUESTIONS.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/PAPERCUTS.md differs from the view this build would emit (7 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/REVIEW-REQUIRED.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-E011]: docs/generated/ROADMAP.md differs from the view this build would emit (2 differing lines) note: line 2 committed: "     source-graph: sha256:c82e0f48261e88c773f1b5577fc28749741b8ddae08d16870c52ff7926c37bd3" note: line 2 emitted:   "     source-graph: sha256:ea9d81f6f7a5605360578f777296de18812136734cb1d05e1db19b5f19ccffdc" help: run `akr build` and commit the result; see @sys.policy.no-hand-edited-views (see V-112) error[AKR-R052]: akr.lock has no hash for akr.papercut.collated-18-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/1 --> .akr/records/akr/papercuts.akr:180:1 | 180 | record akr.papercut.collated-18-papercuts-from-evidence-intake/1 : papercut { | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ | 8 errors rejected all generated views because they embedded commit '(none)' while the clean workspace now resolves the new commit hash. Committing the regenerated headers would create another hash, so the documented atomic path needs commit-stable view metadata or a post-commit strategy that converges.

        ## AKR @akr.papercut.collated-1-papercuts-from-evidence-intake [about akr]
        Collated 1 papercut from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects on 2026-08-09 (D-030). Each source key is also in the collated slot, which is the dedup set for the next run; the sisters were read and never written. ## Evidence-intake-project @evidence-intake-project.papercut.ran-knowledge-context-goal-on-a-project-whose [about akr] Ran knowledge.context --goal on a project whose ledger held only the namespace stub in project.akr; it failed with AKR-X001 "does not resolve to a record" rather than saying the ledger is empty. On a fresh project that reads as a broken tool call. A distinct message ("no records yet; start with knowledge.propose") would tell an agent to author the first record instead of retrying with different goal strings.

        ## AKR @akr.papercut.collated-10-papercuts-from-evidence-intake [about akr]
        Collated 6 AKR papercuts from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects on 2026-08-09 (D-030). Four untagged Lege-ecosystem papercuts were mistakenly absorbed by the previous revision and are excluded here; each retained source key is in the collated slot. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.after-committing-validated-akr-record-changes [about akr] After committing validated AKR record changes with ordinary Git, `akr lock --check` rejected the embedded build commit because the lock was generated against the parent source commit. The workflow needs an atomic documented commit path or an explicit follow-up lock-only commit; otherwise a seemingly clean knowledge commit fails the required post-commit audit. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.at-handoff-knowledge-validate-withheld-its [about akr] At handoff, knowledge.validate withheld its entire 8.5k-token result and suggested narrower arguments even though the tool exposes no scope/filter argument; diagnosing required the separate `akr check` CLI (the protocol's `knowledge.validate` name is not a CLI subcommand). A summarized diagnostics mode would make the required handoff check actionable. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.correcting-an-inaccurate-papercut-via-knowledge [about akr] Correcting an inaccurate papercut via knowledge.supersede failed because the connected workspace vocabulary 0.1 does not support the tool schema's `new_key` option. The correction had to reuse the original key instead of creating a clearer replacement key. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.creating-the-finalization-work-record-failed [about akr] Creating the finalization work record failed because `depends_on` cannot reference a completed work record, but the propose schema does not identify which relation should capture historical provenance. The write was retried with only the live decision dependency. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.knowledge-context-withheld-a-4-1k-token-bundle [about akr] knowledge.context withheld a 4.1k-token bundle even though I requested a 16k budget, then advised requesting fewer tokens. The MCP response ceiling is lower than the tool's advertised budget parameter, so context had to be retried with narrower paths. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.while-recording-the-renderer-decision-knowledge [about akr] While recording the renderer decision, knowledge.propose rejected a dotted decision topic even though the key is dot-delimited; the topic segment syntax was not apparent from the tool schema, so the write had to be retried with a single hyphenated segment. The response also warned that the connected MCP vocabulary 0.2 is newer than the workspace build vocabulary 0.1.

        ## AKR @akr.papercut.collated-16-papercuts-from-kitchen-concept-lege [about akr]
        Collated 16 papercuts from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects on 2026-08-14 (D-030). Each source key is also in the collated slot, which is the dedup set for the next run; the sisters were read and never written. ## Kitchen-Concept @kitchen.papercut.akr-mcp-server-failed-to-connect-at-session [about akr] AKR MCP server failed to connect at session start, so knowledge.start/context were unavailable; used the akr CLI instead. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.akr-papercut-collate-defaults-to-scanning [about akr] akr papercut collate defaults to scanning sibling workspaces of the current root, so running it in Lege-ecosystem looks like it should list this folder's cuts but instead walks Rust-projects/*. Local review is akr view papercuts; collate is an aggregator. Defaulting --projects to the current workspace (and requiring --projects .. to scan sisters) would match the obvious meaning. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.akr-propose-from-rejected-a-yaml-like-statement [about akr] akr propose --from rejected a YAML-like statement: fragment with expected a value, found :. The accepted shape is an AKR slot-list (statement """ ... """), which CLI help does not show. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.knowledge-evidence-add-many-rejected-an [about akr] knowledge.evidence_add_many rejected an otherwise valid evidence batch because the repository shorthand `lege` is not the declared `lege-ecosystem` namespace; surfacing valid namespaces in the write error or start bundle would avoid the retry. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.knowledge-papercut-failed-on-first-use-because [about akr] knowledge.papercut failed on first use because this multi-namespace workspace required an explicit namespace, but knowledge.start did not surface the available namespace choices in its session head. Including them in the handoff would prevent the retry. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.knowledge-start-returned-a-2350-token-compact [about akr] knowledge.start returned a 2350-token compact preview that exceeded its own 2000-token hard limit and required a second narrower call; honoring the requested/default hard limit on the first response would avoid the retry. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem.papercut.while-proposing-a-work-record-the-callable [about akr] While proposing a work record, the callable schema did not expose that `intent` is mandatory or that `topic` is invalid for planning kinds; the validator rejected the otherwise structured call. Including kind-specific required slots and field applicability in tool metadata would avoid the retry. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.akr-build-fails-on-this-working-tree-with-error [about akr] `akr build` fails on this working tree with "error[AKR-I002]: cannot write revisions: CHECK constraint failed: method IS NULL OR method IN ('manual','command','observation','instrumented')". It succeeds against HEAD, so one of the uncommitted record edits in .akr/records/saveyourskin/ carries a method value outside that set. The error names neither the record nor the offending value, which makes it hard to find; reporting the key and method would fix that. Consequence: the lock cannot be refreshed, so knowledge.validate reports AKR-R052 for every newly written record. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.akr-mcp-server-failed-to-connect-used-the-akr [about akr] AKR MCP server failed to connect; used the akr CLI for start/context/propose/validate. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.knowledge-get-rendered-a-work-record-s-main [about akr] knowledge.get rendered a work record's main slot without naming it, so a faithful knowledge.revise first tried `goal`; the schema only then revealed the slot is `intent`. Exposing slot names in body/canonical output would avoid a failed write attempt. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.knowledge-propose-accepted-an-implements [about akr] knowledge.propose accepted an `implements` relation to a work record at the call boundary and only later rejected it under V-005; relation target-kind constraints in the tool description would make completed-slice recording single-pass. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.knowledge-propose-accepted-observation-method [about akr] knowledge.propose accepted observation method `inspection` and wrote a verified revision, but `akr build` rejects that stored historical revision because the index CHECK allows only manual/command/observation/instrumented. Revising the head to `instrumented` cannot repair the now-invalid sealed revision, so write-time validation and the index schema need the same enum or a supported repair path. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.while-recording-a-completed-work-slice [about akr] While recording a completed work slice, knowledge.propose's schema exposed slots as an unconstrained object and did not tell me that work records accept intent/target/note rather than summary; the call failed only after validation. Publishing per-kind slot names in the tool schema or description would avoid the retry. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.while-recording-a-verified-gameplay-video [about akr] While recording a verified gameplay-video observation, knowledge.propose parsed a descriptive method containing `15 fps` and punctuation as slot syntax instead of rejecting it as an invalid enum value. The schema should validate/quote the method argument before serializing it. ## jpegXL-rs @jpegxl-rs.papercut.while-checking-an-in-progress-akr-change-akr [about akr] While checking an in-progress AKR change, `akr change status` was rejected because the CLI exposes `change show` instead; a status alias or help hint would make this transition easier to discover. ## orderflower @orderflower.papercut.on-a-freshly-initialised-repo-with-no-commits [about akr] On a freshly initialised repo with no commits yet, knowledge.evidence_add and knowledge.papercut both fail with "no commit to record: not inside a git repository". That blocks knowledge.complete for every acceptance check at exactly the moment a first milestone finishes, and the message points at the wrong cause: git init had been run, there simply were no commits. A message naming the real fix (make an initial commit, or pass observed_at) would have saved a detour.

        ## AKR @akr.papercut.collated-18-papercuts-from-evidence-intake [about akr]
        Collated 18 papercuts from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects on 2026-08-11 (D-030). Each source key is also in the collated slot, which is the dedup set for the next run; the sisters were read and never written. ## Evidence-intake-project @evidence-intake-project.papercut.i-passed-scope-terms-as-path-src-gui-winsafe-rs [about akr] I passed scope terms as "path src/gui/winsafe.rs" to knowledge.propose, copying the `path "..."` form the generated views render, and it was accepted silently — producing scope `path "path src/gui/winsafe.rs"`, a glob that can never match a file, so the records would never have gone stale when winsafe.rs changed. Nothing validates that a scope glob is well-formed or warns that it currently matches zero paths. A warning when a path term matches nothing in the worktree would have caught this at propose time instead of three records later. ## Evidence-intake-project @evidence-intake-project.papercut.knowledge-explain-crashed-with-akr-x099 [about akr] knowledge.explain crashed with AKR-X099 (internal) for both `decision` and `constraint` after knowledge.propose rejected my slots with AKR-T002. The propose error names the bad slot but not the valid ones, and explain -- the documented way to find them -- is exactly what fails, so I had to read .akr/records/*.akr by hand to learn that a decision uses `decision` plus `claim` blocks and has no `rationale` or `note` slot. Suggest AKR-T002 list the kind's valid slots inline. ## Evidence-intake-project @evidence-intake-project.papercut.knowledge-propose-returns-lock-stale-true-on [about akr] knowledge.propose returns lock_stale:true on every write but does not refresh akr.lock, so a later knowledge.validate fails with AKR-R052 ("akr.lock has no hash for <the record I just wrote>"). The fix is `akr lock --update`, but nothing in the propose result or the diagnostic says so, and AGENTS.md's "never hand-edit .akr/" made me hesitant to touch the lock at all. Suggest either refreshing the lock as part of propose, or having AKR-R052 name `akr lock --update` as the remedy. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.after-several-successful-akr-writes-the-mcp [about akr] After several successful AKR writes, the MCP transport closed persistently: knowledge.explain and knowledge.revise both failed immediately, and error[AKR-C004]: "D-011" is neither a registered diagnostic code, a known rule, nor a record kind also rejected a decision identifier even though tool guidance names it. The local  fallback completed the handoff update. ## Lege-ecosystem @lege-ecosystem-perf.papercut.knowledge-papercut-rejected-its-first-call [about akr] `knowledge.papercut` rejected its first call because this multi-namespace project requires an explicit namespace, although the task protocol's example does not mention that requirement. Retrying with `namespace: lege-ecosystem-perf` succeeded. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.after-knowledge-revise-changed-an-existing [about akr] After knowledge.revise changed an existing milestone revision from proposed to ready in place, a second revise with the same base revision and state active created a new revision in proposed state instead of activating it. This repeated on revisions 3 and 4; completing directly from ready worked. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.authoring-a-new-track-s-records-cost-three [about akr] Authoring a new track's records cost three failed writes to discover relation/slot rules that aren't visible from the propose schema: a `constraint` rejects the `note` slot (planning kinds only, D-026), an `observation` may not declare `supported_by`, and a `milestone` may not declare `implements` (both V-005). The tool description lists relation names generically, so the per-kind restrictions only surface as errors after a full record body is composed. Either the propose schema's relations field could name the allowed relations per kind, or the V-005 error could suggest the nearest legal relation (`derived_from` in both of my cases). Separately, an `observation`'s `observed_at` requires a 40-hex commit while the kind's description just says "observed_at", so an ISO date is a natural first guess and fails with a parse cascade of three diagnostics. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.knowledge-context-rejected-a-valid-key-revision [about akr] knowledge.context rejected a valid @key/revision reference even though knowledge.get accepts that canonical reference form; it required the bare logical key, making goal resumption take two retries. The context tool/docs could state or normalize this syntax. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.knowledge-supersede-documents-a-new-key-option [about akr] knowledge.supersede documents a new_key option, but AKR 0.1 rejects every different-key supersession with AKR-C004. I had to supersede the existing key in place, which prevents giving a materially changed design ruling a more accurate key. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.revising-an-active-decision-s-content [about akr] Revising an active decision's content implicitly returned its lifecycle state to proposed, requiring a second state-only revise. Preserving the head state by default, or explicitly warning that omitted state resets it, would avoid an easy accidental deactivation. ## SaveYourSkin @saveyourskin.papercut.while-removing-37-acknowledged-missing-source [about akr] While removing 37 acknowledged missing-source citations, knowledge.get, knowledge.revise, knowledge.explain, and knowledge.validate repeatedly returned Transport closed; the local akr CLI remained healthy and provided the necessary fallback. ## jpegXL-rs @jpegxl-rs.papercut.knowledge-propose-documents-observation [about akr] knowledge.propose documents observation observed_at as required but does not say it must be a git:<40-hex> locator; passing the natural ISO date 2026-08-11 produced parser errors about a 4-digit commit hash. ## jpegXL-rs @jpegxl-rs.papercut.knowledge-propose-says-observation-requires [about akr] knowledge.propose says observation requires statement and observed_at, but a verified observation also requires method/source/evidence (V-022); after adding method, the natural accompanying command slot is rejected because observation does not permit command. ## raw-autotune @raw-autotune.papercut.akr-explain-assessment-lists-optional-as-of-but [about akr] akr explain assessment lists optional as_of but not that it requires a git commit; using an ISO date caused a safe parse failure. Showing slot types in explain would avoid this retry. ## raw-autotune @raw-autotune.papercut.akr-propose-from-rejected-a-prose-body-as-does [about akr] akr propose --from rejected a prose body as 'does not parse as a record'; the CLI help calls it a body source but this build requires a complete canonical record. ## raw-autotune @raw-autotune.papercut.knowledge-explain-observation-and-knowledge [about akr] knowledge.explain observation and knowledge.papercut both failed with Transport closed while recording the sky-stage diagnosis; the local akr CLI worked as a fallback. ## raw-autotune @raw-autotune.papercut.the-akr-cli-search-syntax-differs-from-the-mcp [about akr] The AKR CLI search syntax differs from the MCP shape: --query is rejected and the help must be consulted separately. ## raw-autotune @raw-autotune.papercut.the-knowledge-context-mcp-connector-returned [about akr] The knowledge.context MCP connector returned 'Transport closed' at the start of implementation; using the equivalent akr CLI workflow.

